// Formative practice quiz for merge-conflicts. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design.
$CATEGORY: git-linux-ai-foundations/git-foundations/merge-conflicts

::merge-conflicts o1 - when Git does not stop::You merge feature into main. feature changed only footer.html, and main changed only header.html since they split. What does Git do?{
=Combines both changes on its own, with no conflict#Correct. Git stops only when it cannot combine the changes automatically, commonly because both branches changed the same area. Separate files merge automatically.
~Stops with a conflict, because both branches have new commits#Not quite. New commits on both sides are normal for a merge. Git stops only when it cannot combine the changes automatically, commonly when both changed the same area.
~Stops with a conflict, because both branches changed HTML files#Not quite. The file type does not matter. These are different files, so nothing overlaps.
~Keeps only the newer of the two changes#Not quite. Git never picks a winner by date. It keeps both changes here because they do not overlap.
}

::merge-conflicts o2 - name the side inside the markers::You are on release and run git merge hotfix. Both changed line 5 of price.txt. In the conflict block, which version sits between the marker line that starts with seven less-than signs and says HEAD, and the separator line of seven equals signs?{
=release's version, the branch you are on#Correct. HEAD is your current branch. Its version comes first, and the incoming branch's version follows the separator.
~hotfix's version, the branch being merged in#Not quite. The incoming branch's version comes after the separator and ends at the marker line naming hotfix.
~Whichever version was committed more recently#Not quite. The order depends on which branch you are on, not on dates.
~The text of line 5 before either branch changed it#Not quite. In Git's default conflict style the block shows the two branches' versions, not the original text.
}

::merge-conflicts o3 - finish after editing::During a merge you edited the conflicted file to its final text and deleted all three marker lines. git status still lists the file as unmerged. What do you do next?{
=git add the file, then git commit to finish the merge#Correct. git add marks the conflict resolved, and git commit creates the merge commit.
~git merge --abort, to save your resolution#Not quite. --abort cancels the merge and restores the pre-merge state, so your resolution would be lost.
~git commit straight away; the file does not need adding#Not quite. Git will not commit while a file is still unmerged. git add is how you tell Git it is resolved.
~Run the same git merge command again#Not quite. A merge is already in progress. Running it again does not finish it; git add and git commit do.
}

::merge-conflicts o3 - leftover markers::A day after you resolved a conflict and committed, the web page shows a line of seven equals signs. What went wrong?{
=A marker line was left in the file, and Git let you commit it#Correct. Git does not check the file contents for markers. Check that all three marker lines are gone before git add.
~Git always adds a separator line to merged files#Not quite. Git adds marker lines only while a conflict is unresolved. Removing them is your job.
~Git would have refused the commit, so a teammate must have added it#Not quite. Git lets you commit leftover markers, so the resolver can miss them.
~The merge was cancelled with git merge --abort#Not quite. --abort restores the pre-merge state and leaves no markers behind.
}
